//
// `(T-Absent)` names `t == null` and `t ?? d` as ONE question — and only `??` had an answer.
//
// `t == null` fell past every gate in the comparison lowering (`vec_null`, `float_null`,
// `enum_null`, `ref_null`, `senum_null` — no tuple among them) and was refused outright:
// *"No matching operator '==' on '(integer?, integer?)' and 'null'"*, for a type whose `??`
// beside it worked.  So the language had a null question a tuple could be asked one way and
// not the other.
//
// The arm asks `coalesce_not_null` and negates it rather than restating the member walk: two
// spellings of one question that each carried their own walk is exactly the drift `(T-Absent)`
// forbids, and it is what let the first-member convention live in one of them unnoticed
// (`1477`, the `??` half).
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  which member is absent (all · first-only · none), the operator
//                        (`==` · `!=`), and how the tuple ARRIVES — a written literal, whose
//                        type is `(τ?, τ?)`, and an INDEX MISS, whose type is the in-flight
//                        `(τ, τ)?`.  Those are two different spellings of one type under
//                        `(T-Absent)`'s equivalence and the gate matches on `base()` so both
//                        reach the arm; a fix that named only the written spelling passes
//                        every cell but the last two.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the `??` spelling is `1477`'s.
//
// @falsified-at: 4f29a473 — the file does not COMPILE there: `--tests` reports
//   `(parse errors)`, `1 failed; 0 passed`, so not one assertion runs.  That is the whole
//   reason this is its own file: merged into `1477` it would have refused before a single `??`
//   assertion executed, and the `??` half would have been falsified by a compile error instead
//   of by its own wrong answers — a guard whose two halves fail on the same channel cannot say
//   which of them the cure fixed
//   CHANNEL: the exit channel, measured on --interpret against 4f29a473 by
//   `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk`.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

// One test function per cell, so a `--tests` run names every broken one rather than halting.

fn test_all_null_is_null() {
  d: (integer?, integer?) = (null, null);
  assert(d == null, "all-null is null");
}

fn test_a_present_member_makes_it_not_null() {
  a: (integer?, integer?) = (null, 2);
  assert(!(a == null), "first-null with a present member is NOT null");
}

fn test_wholly_present_is_not_null() {
  c: (integer?, integer?) = (1, 2);
  assert(!(c == null), "wholly present is not null");
}

fn test_not_equal_is_the_negation() {
  a: (integer?, integer?) = (null, 2);
  d: (integer?, integer?) = (null, null);
  assert(a != null, "`!=` is the negation");
  assert(!(d != null), "`!=` on an all-null tuple");
}

// The IN-FLIGHT spelling: an index miss types `(integer, integer)?`, which `(T-Absent)` makes
// the same type as the written `(integer?, integer?)` above.  The gate matches on `base()` so
// both reach the arm; a fix naming only the written spelling passes every cell but these.
fn test_the_in_flight_spelling_answers_too() {
  v: vector<(integer, integer)> = [(1, 2)];
  miss = 5;
  hit = 0;
  assert(v[miss] == null, "an index miss is null");
  assert(!(v[hit] == null), "an in-range read is not null");
}
